  "query_preview": "..\n\nMid clouds have been expanding across the area today as a mid level \nwave rides east along a warm front across IN/OH later this \nafternoon. The wave was boosted this afternoon by a low level jet \nlifting north into the area which will continue northward while the \nwave pulls away to the east. Over the last few days, models have \ncontinually highlighted the potential for weak showers to accompany \nthe warm front as it lifts through southern MI after 00Z tonight. \nThough there will be separation between two of the main features, \nthere is still a tight theta e gradient that will lift through the \narea tonight on the front that could touch off some showers. PWATs \nhave been increasing over the last few runs as well with a narrow \nwindow of close to 1 inch lifting through. A slight chance of \nshowers was added in the last forecast cycle which will be only \nslightly adjusted this go around, mainly broadening it due to \ndiscrepancies in timing of the likely narrow band of showers. Even \nwithin 12 hours the CAMs differ by 3-4 hours with timing of the \nfeature. Any precip will lift north likely exiting the forecast area \nby 12Z Saturday. \n\nWarmer temps still likely on Saturday as southerly flow ramps up \nbehind the warm front positioned over northern MI. Thermal ridge \nfolding into the area has settled around 17C at 850mb by the time it \ngets here with the warmest part of the airmass lifting north and \nwest of the area through MN. Thus chances of hitting 80F for highs \nhave gone down in favor of mid to upper 70s. \n\nExtensive dry spell looks to come to an end Sunday as the remnants \nof Hurricane Isaias lifts northward toward the Ohio Valley. Recent \ntrends keep the low center more to the south over KY before whisking \nit off to the east but the expansive precip shield will still work \nup through most or all of SE MI Saturday night into Sunday. PWATs \ncontinue to climb with the system and we're now up to around 1.5 \ninches across most of the area but the western part of lower MI will \nbe fighting drier air and ridging building into the area so will \nprobably see a good cutoff of rain across western lower. Currently \nQPF totals are around a quarter to half inch across the area. \n\nPeriodic rain chances continue through the end of the upcoming week \nas a deep upper level trough sets up over the western conus ejecting \nseveral waves which look to phase with northern stream troughs \nworking across southern Canada. First chances look to be with a cold \nfront Monday night into Tuesday with another front Friday.",
